{{Japanese name|Seda}}

'''[[Heitarō Seda]]''' (瀬田 平太郎, ''Seda Heitarō''; born 8 June 1948) is a Sanese politician who served as the 50th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 2009 to 2011. A veteran legislator from the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), he is often characterized by historians as the "Last Guardian of the Republic," representing the final administration before the [[Jutsu Restoration]] fundamentally altered the constitutional structure of the nation.

Before ascending to the premiership, Seda built a reputation as Okaiken's foremost defense policy expert. He served two non-consecutive terms as [[Minister of Defence (Okaiken)|Minister of Defence]] and held the post of [[Deputy Prime Minister of Okaiken|Deputy Prime Minister]] for five years under [[Kenshin Hirota]]. His tenure as Prime Minister was dominated by the ratification of the [[United Sovereign Nations]] (USNW) accession treaty and the management of a fragile economic recovery. He resigned abruptly in January 2011 following a stroke, paving the way for the rise of [[Tadahisa Jōhei]].

==Early life and education==
Seda was born in [[Morioka]], [[Iwate Prefecture]], into a family of sake brewers. The region had historically been a stronghold of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP) and was the home district of former radical Prime Minister [[Genzui Kusaka]]. Growing up in this politically charged environment shaped Seda's lifelong commitment to conservative stability.

He attended [[Okami University]], graduating with a degree in Economics in 1971. Rather than entering the bureaucracy, Seda worked for a heavy industries trading firm, spending several years in the company's offices in [[Shayden]]. This experience solidified his belief in the necessity of the Sanese-Shayden security alliance.

==Political career==
Seda entered politics in 1993, running for the House of Commoners in the Iwate 2nd District. He successfully unseated the incumbent Socialist, symbolizing the LDP's resurgence in the rural north during the "Tomita Restoration" era.

He quickly distinguished himself as a "defense hawk" within the party's policy research council. In 2001, Prime Minister [[Katsuya Okada]] appointed him Minister of Defence. During his first term, Seda oversaw the modernization of the Maritime Self-Defense Force and advocated for a more active interpretation of Article 9 to allow for peacekeeping operations.

Between 2006 and 2009, Seda served as the loyal Deputy Prime Minister to [[Kenshin Hirota]]. He acted as the administration's primary crisis manager, coordinating the government's response to the 2008 global financial shock and managing the politically sensitive Truth and Reconciliation Commission regarding the Great Adonian War.

==Prime Minister (2009–2011)==
Following [[Kenshin Hirota]]'s resignation in October 2009, Seda was elected President of the LDP and designated Prime Minister. He was viewed as a continuity candidate who would protect the legacy of the Hirota administration while preparing the party for the next general election.

===USNW Accession===
The defining achievement of the Seda Cabinet was the finalization of Okaiken's entry into the [[United Sovereign Nations]] (USNW). Building on the diplomatic groundwork laid by his Foreign Minister [[Tadahisa Jōhei]], Seda pushed the accession treaty through a divided Diet in late 2010. He argued that economic integration with the Western bloc was the only way to secure Okaiken's future against the rising influence of [[Peilan]].

===Domestic Policy===
Domestically, Seda pursued a policy of fiscal caution. He resisted calls from the LDP's nationalist wing to engage in massive deficit spending, preferring targeted subsidies for the technology sector. His "Digital Okaiken" initiative is credited with laying the infrastructure for the country's modern 5G network, although its immediate economic impact was muted.

His administration was frequently criticized for being "bureaucratic" and lacking a grand vision. With the economy stagnating and the "En Crisis" looming on the horizon, public dissatisfaction with the LDP's slow pace of reform began to mount.

===Resignation===
On the evening of 2 January 2011, Seda collapsed at his private residence in Setagaya. He was rushed to the Okami University Hospital where he was diagnosed with a mild cerebral hemorrhage. Although he regained consciousness and doctors expected a full recovery, Seda decided that he could not effectively lead the nation during his rehabilitation.

On 9 January 2011, barely a week after his collapse, he formally resigned as Prime Minister. He endorsed his Foreign Minister, [[Tadahisa Jōhei]], as his successor, unaware that Jōhei would soon dismantle the republican framework Seda had spent his career defending.

==Later life==
Seda made a full recovery and returned to the Diet later in 2011. He watched with private skepticism as Prime Minister Jōhei executed the [[Jutsu Restoration]], reportedly telling colleagues that restoring the Emperor's head-of-state status was "opening a door that should have remained closed."

Despite his reservations, he remained a loyal party man and did not publicly oppose the 2011 referendums. He retired from politics in 2014, choosing not to run in the general election that solidified the power of [[Toshiro Kurosawa]]. He currently lives in retirement in Morioka, serving as an honorary advisor to the Sanese Defense Academy.

